Round 5 (a7ec33b, PR #59 open, not merged). Finding on 74c7ee5: the waiting round's recursive LOCK TABLE on a partitioned table held it while waiting for a partition, so a partition reader turning writer through the partitioned table deadlocked with apply (40P01).

Fix:
- lockable_tables_in_lock_order expands each partitioned table into its partition tree, ordinary and partitioned members only.
- The tree is walked through pg_inherits; a probe showed pg_partition_tree holds ACCESS SHARE on every partition until commit.
- Members are ordered by depth, then name, each in the strongest mode any entry needs.
- Every member gets its own LOCK TABLE ONLY [NOWAIT], so each wait is on one relation while apply holds none of the set.
- Foreign partitions, which LOCK TABLE ONLY refuses, stay out of the set. This residual reaches plain row triggers only: probed, a constraint trigger cannot exist on such a tree.

The probe (pg_locks) shows the expanded set equals the row trigger statements' footprint for a tree without foreign partitions.

New live cases:
- managed_trigger_drops_wait_for_one_partition_at_a_time
- managed_trigger_retirements_wait_for_one_partition_at_a_time

Every lock-order race now also requires that apply, while it waits, holds no schema table except its ledger.

Mutation controls:
- recursive form: both new cases fail with 40P01;
- pg_partition_tree: the six partition races fail on held ACCESS SHARE;
- blocking acquisition in partition order: the across-partition case and both new cases fail with 40P01;
- SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE targets excluded: the mixed case fails with 40P01;
- every declared trigger's table locked: both idempotence cases wait.

Verification (Linux, Rust 1.98.1, PostgreSQL 18.6):
- cargo test -p batter-sqlx --all-features --locked: pass;
- managed_objects_live: 38/38; the inventory matches 38 managed cases and 180 in total;
- lifecycle example: 4/4 live, and the SIGTERM and SIGINT smokes pass;
- bash scripts/verify.sh: passed (exit 0);
- hosted CI at a7ec33b: all 7 checks passed (verify 1.94.0 20m20s, verify 1.98.1 20m40s, Native Runlimit PostgreSQL x2, macOS subprocess x2, file budgets).

Residuals are listed in the report: foreign partitions, statement-level over-locking, the tree changing before the first lock round, the untested 32-round cap, and locks outside the set.

Round 6 (18d1507, PR #59 open, not merged). Three findings on a7ec33b.

1. Concurrent partition attachment (medium). The trees were read once, so a partition attached during the lock wait was missing from the set. The later DROP TRIGGER then waited for it while holding the rest (40P01).
   Fix: take_all reads the set again once a round holds all of it, and on a change rolls the round back and takes the new set. ATTACH, CREATE PARTITION OF, DETACH and DROP lock the partitioned table, and RENAME locks the partition (probed), so the read stays exact while the set is held. Lock-set churn in the last of the 32 rounds returns Capacity, with its rustdoc broadened (no new variant).
   New case: managed_trigger_lock_set_follows_a_partition_attached_during_its_wait.

2. Lock privileges (medium). Each LOCK TABLE needs MAINTAIN, UPDATE, DELETE or TRUNCATE on its relation, partitions included. The trigger statements need less there: CREATE TRIGGER needs TRIGGER, and DROP TRIGGER ownership of the named table only (probed).
   Decision: the locks stay and Batter grants nothing. The requirement and an owner-side GRANT MAINTAIN example are documented in the rustdoc, README, ADR-013, AGENTS, integrations and CHANGELOG (as a behaviour change).
   New cases: trigger_privileges.rs (TRIGGER-only login; separately owned partition). Each fails with 42501 with the catalog unchanged, then succeeds after GRANT MAINTAIN.

3. Stale testing guide (low): the case counts and the commit-together description are corrected.

Mutation controls on this head:
- second read skipped: the attach case fails with 40P01;
- lock step removed: both privilege cases apply without the grant;
- recursive form, pg_partition_tree, blocking acquisition in partition order, SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE targets excluded, every declared table locked: each still fails its target cases.

Verification (Linux, Rust 1.98.1, PostgreSQL 18.6):
- cargo test -p batter-sqlx --all-features --locked: 287 passed;
- managed_objects_live: 41/41, and the inventory matches 41 managed cases and 183 in total;
- lifecycle example: 4/4 live, and the SIGTERM and SIGINT smokes pass;
- bash scripts/verify.sh: passed (exit 0) on the committed tree;
- hosted CI at 18d1507: all 7 checks passed (verify 1.94.0 19m42s, verify 1.98.1 19m46s, Native Runlimit PostgreSQL x2, macOS subprocess x2, file budgets); a7ec33b had all 7 checks green.

New residual (probed): a partition dropped or renamed while apply waits for that very partition fails the wait with 42P01, and apply rolls back.

Round 7 (0731b2d, PR #59 open, not merged). Two owner findings on 18d1507, handled as a further round per the orchestrator.

1. Recomputed pending work could bypass the acquired locks (medium). After a trigger retirement, the second catalog read could add work on a managed trigger that another session had replaced: a drop on a table outside the set, or one held only in SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE mode. Apply then waited for that table while holding the retired trigger's table (40P01). Both new cases reproduced this on 18d1507's code before the fix.
   Fix: lock_trigger_tables returns the held LockSet (relation and mode). After the second read, require_covered walks each pending trigger's tree without locking and requires every member held, in ACCESS EXCLUSIVE mode when a drop is needed. Uncovered work fails with IdentityMismatch (rustdoc broadened) before apply requests another lock. The retirements roll back and are not replayed; clone handling is unchanged.
   New cases: trigger_lock_coverage.rs (unlocked table; stronger lock needed).

2. CHANGELOG "additive" claim (low): now limited to the Rust API, naming the READ COMMITTED pin and the lock privileges as runtime changes.

Mutation controls on this head:
- coverage check skipped: both coverage cases fail with 40P01;
- modes ignored: only the stronger-lock case fails;
- every earlier control (second read skipped, lock step removed, blocking acquisition, SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE excluded, every declared table, pg_partition_tree, 74c7ee5 lock code): each still fails its target cases.

Verification (Linux, Rust 1.98.1, PostgreSQL 18.6):
- cargo test -p batter-sqlx --all-features --locked: 287 passed;
- managed_objects_live: 43/43, and the inventory matches 43 managed cases and 185 in total;
- lifecycle example: 4/4 live, and the SIGTERM and SIGINT smokes pass;
- bash scripts/verify.sh: passed (exit 0) on the committed tree;
- hosted CI at 0731b2d: all 7 checks passed (verify 1.94.0 15m25s, verify 1.98.1 19m49s, Native Runlimit PostgreSQL x2, macOS subprocess x2, file budgets); 18d1507 had all 7 checks green.

Round 8 (7f26d06, PR #59 open, not merged). One owner finding on 0731b2d.

Coverage validation repeated catalog queries while holding table locks (low, performance). require_covered ran one recursive walk per pending trigger, repeating shared tables, while ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locks blocked readers.
Confirmed by inspection and a probe: 600 walks over 200 tables cost 293-412 ms, against 71-87 ms batched through psql over loopback.

Fix: catalog::partition_tree_members walks all pending trigger tables in one query, each distinct table once. The pure first_uncovered keeps per-entry modes and reports the first uncovered entry in manifest order, still before any uncovered work runs.

New unit tests (managed/apply/drops/tests.rs): modes over shared tables, first uncovered entry, every tree member, tables without members.

Mutation controls on this head:
- mode-blind: the mode unit test and the live stronger-lock case fail (40P01);
- last uncovered reported: the ordering unit test fails;
- only the first member checked: the tree unit test fails;
- walk rows discarded: both live coverage cases fail (40P01).

Verification:
- cargo test -p batter-sqlx --all-features --locked: 291 passed;
- managed_objects_live: 43/43;
- lifecycle example: 4/4 live, and the SIGTERM and SIGINT smokes pass;
- bash scripts/verify.sh: passed (exit 0) on the committed tree;
- hosted CI at 7f26d06: all 7 checks passed (verify 1.94.0 19m25s, verify 1.98.1 19m35s, Native Runlimit PostgreSQL x2, macOS subprocess x2, file budgets).

Round 9 (996a657, PR #59 open, not merged). One owner finding on 7f26d06.

Duplicate requests multiplied partition-tree work while locks were held (medium, performance). Each pending trigger and retirement seeded the recursive lock-set query separately, and duplicates were merged only after expansion.
Confirmed by a probe: 1,000 requests for one table with 1,000 partitions expanded 1,001,000 recursive rows in 3.07 s, against 1,001 rows in 8 ms for one request.

Fix: lock_trigger_tables coalesces requests by schema and table, keeping the strongest mode. lockable_tables_in_lock_order now takes only that map, so each table reaches the walk once by construction. The acquisition and revalidation protocols are unchanged.

New unit test: lock_requests_coalesce_by_table_keeping_the_strongest_mode.
Control: keeping the first request's mode fails that test and deadlocks managed_trigger_drops_precede_replacements_on_one_table (40P01).

Verification:
- cargo test -p batter-sqlx --all-features --locked: 292 passed;
- managed_objects_live: 43/43;
- lifecycle example: 4/4 live, and the SIGTERM and SIGINT smokes pass;
- bash scripts/verify.sh: passed (exit 0) on the committed tree;
- hosted CI at 996a657: all 7 checks passed (verify 1.94.0 16m59s, verify 1.98.1 19m21s, Native Runlimit PostgreSQL x2, macOS subprocess x2, file budgets).
